WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.417
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
This week, we're

00:00:01.417 --> 00:00:03.802
talking about Kubernetes,
cloud native, and networking.

00:00:03.802 --> 00:00:05.760
Welcome to "This Week in
Cloud," where we serve

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.260
you the lowest latency news.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:10.710
We recently talked about how
Kubernetes apps in the GCP

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.320
Marketplace make it
easier to find and deploy

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.477
commercial or open-source apps.

00:00:15.477 --> 00:00:17.310
Managing or building
Kubernetes applications

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.110
takes a lot of
effort, which is why

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:21.210
Kubernetes operators
help standardize and make

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.560
the process easier.

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.630
The application Custom
Resource Definition

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.810
includes a standard API for
creating, viewing, and managing

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.510
applications in Kubernetes,
giving you well-defined ways

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:33.360
to interact with and
manage your deployed apps.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.250
Read more about these
Kubernetes operators

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.430
and see them in action
on the GCP Marketplace.

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.560
After all, Kubernetes
has played a major role

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:42.910
in helping developers
build cloud native.

00:00:42.910 --> 00:00:45.210
But there's many projects
that have been redeveloped

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:48.000
and released in the open-source
community, such as Istio,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.593
Knative and Kubeflow.

00:00:49.593 --> 00:00:51.510
Check out this blog post
that talks more about

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.760
what these tools do, and
how they can help you

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.220
build a cloud-native future.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.960
For a more traditional
application,

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.912
cloud native can sometimes
feel overwhelming.

00:00:59.912 --> 00:01:02.370
If you're familiar with any
application that's one part web

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.507
server and one part database,
such as a traditional AMP app,

00:01:05.507 --> 00:01:07.215
check out this new
solution for migrating

00:01:07.215 --> 00:01:09.603
your two-tier
application to GCP.

00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:11.520
It presents all the
central planning questions

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.312
you'll need to have
answered ahead of time,

00:01:13.312 --> 00:01:16.010
then makes the case for various
compute and database options.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:17.760
If you want to learn
more about migrating,

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:19.950
there's no better
place to start.

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:21.750
Looking to do some
serious networking?

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.670
It takes a lot for
Google's global network

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.597
to handle billions
of users a day.

00:01:25.597 --> 00:01:27.930
And that's the same network
you get on your Google Cloud

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:28.842
projects.

00:01:28.842 --> 00:01:30.300
Check out this
networking deep dive

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:31.925
on the different
load-balancing options

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:33.472
and how Google makes them work.

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:35.430
Keep up to date with more
networking deep dives

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:38.250
on the Google Cloud blog,
and networking end-to-end end

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.395
on the GCP YouTube channel.

00:01:39.395 --> 00:01:41.520
If you want to read more
about these announcements,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.440
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.300
Have any thoughts or ideas,
leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.680
We'll see you in the cloud.

